#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360760 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166829 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c566525f-9b0f-586f-ba60-277400cb25dd" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over a Bounded Interval: Lower Bound is Not Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c1169e5-852d-5544-8468-16696082321e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2287" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357853} {:answer/id #uuid "5822d891-5127-5d79-87f6-93ebd2d0cc13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2481" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358516} {:answer/id #uuid "7e31eb69-f028-5ff2-ab22-1b1d00adfaa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4166" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358851} {:answer/id #uuid "92be8b10-a5ad-5000-8627-169d851892c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3004" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359035} {:answer/id #uuid "e78b968a-3ca0-5854-9293-024e8377fcd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3258" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359804}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5822d891-5127-5d79-87f6-93ebd2d0cc13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2481" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358516} :answer-field/id #uuid "ade7b26b-b2cd-5734-8dc5-4befa7539429" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360300}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a90f56db-3bf1-5904-b2af-586397c0fc70" :question/math-academy-id "q-97155" :question/problem "Given that $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.23),$ compute $P(3<X<7)$ rounded to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.23),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{(0.77)}^{x-1}(0.23), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3<X<7) & =P(X\\in {4,5,6}) \\\\  & =P(X=4)+P(X=5)+P(X=6) \\\\  & =f(4)+f(5)+f(6) \\\\  & ={(0.77)}^{3}(0.23)+{(0.77)}^{4}(0.23)+{(0.77)}^{5}(0.23) \\\\  & ≈0.2481.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360818 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166886 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37fde5f8-b1e8-5903-b875-94b4403cb282" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16838f05-add0-5323-9c0c-9c9c7f9d7436" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2443" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357948} {:answer/id #uuid "17d73f1f-e963-525c-adec-68cc2669194a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2765" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357960} {:answer/id #uuid "3a4194a9-e2ef-5314-8ceb-ee5799230909" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3208" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358264} {:answer/id #uuid "780b701a-c44e-595e-a7c9-5aa54ed3cc1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3557" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358788} {:answer/id #uuid "909da325-b04d-503c-8841-96640c256c29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1792" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359015}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17d73f1f-e963-525c-adec-68cc2669194a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2765" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357960} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcc5e3de-2c6f-5285-9695-0812e0b2622c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360387}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "96496004-4440-5516-85e1-efe7ac7128a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-97464" :question/problem "Given that $X∼B(6,0.6),$ compute $P(X=3).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(6,0.6),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x})(0.6)^{x}(0.4)^{6-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=3) & =f(3) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{3})(0.6)^{3}(0.4)^{6-3} \\\\  & =20(0.6)^{3}(0.4)^{3} \\\\  & ≈0.2765\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $4$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167510 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166888 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f98ff81-db37-590b-8910-58928af97f44" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over a Bounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "205a4120-b56e-594d-9076-bc1a125be7aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1319" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358035} {:answer/id #uuid "23c59bef-5a0b-5a10-8a7a-da0bb2286715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1015" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358068} {:answer/id #uuid "25ddedae-dbd9-5e03-ac96-2463a902c684" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0988" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358093} {:answer/id #uuid "883334a6-a9f2-5375-bd8a-6f7e833ef631" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1523" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358941} {:answer/id #uuid "9d6a14c4-842f-5af3-9522-2283e2126a93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0784" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359127}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25ddedae-dbd9-5e03-ac96-2463a902c684" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0988" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358093} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd92b1aa-5a29-536d-b7bd-9c295dd593e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360347}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "40d3dc98-bb09-5316-943f-68aa25c6d60a" :question/math-academy-id "q-97516" :question/problem "Given $X∼B(6,0.2),$ compute $P(3\\le X\\le 5).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(6,0.2),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x})(0.2)^{x}(0.8)^{6-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3\\le X\\le 5) & =P(X\\in {3,4,5}) \\\\  & =f(3)+f(4)+f(5) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{3})(0.2)^{3}(0.8)^{6-3}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{4})(0.2)^{4}(0.8)^{6-4}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{5})(0.2)^{5}(0.8)^{6-5} \\\\  & =20(0.2)^{3}(0.8)^{3}+15(0.2)^{4}(0.8)^{2}+6(0.2)^{5}(0.8) \\\\  & ≈0.0988\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $4$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362209 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166888 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f98ff81-db37-590b-8910-58928af97f44" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over a Bounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2de3a430-9ad7-57d6-816e-0fdc57ed5566" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3254" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361042} {:answer/id #uuid "a3f33041-53fb-5d9b-8628-63ca3c0dfa83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3783" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361569} {:answer/id #uuid "bc50031e-6b2d-5085-9d9c-b268bb6b587e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4280" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361678} {:answer/id #uuid "e44ffb5f-ad21-57aa-a13f-685c8b26e859" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4520" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361858} {:answer/id #uuid "e9bae9dd-feae-5ee1-b5bf-5da5581fb28e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4960" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9bae9dd-feae-5ee1-b5bf-5da5581fb28e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4960" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361879} :answer-field/id #uuid "979b156b-b4d8-56b6-9e67-a533b7f7f8b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362122}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "00ca7976-03b9-5638-95d5-695719b36e13" :question/math-academy-id "q-97519" :question/problem "Given $X∼B(7,0.9),$ compute $P(5\\le X\\le 6).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial,  we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(7,0.9),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{x})(0.9)^{x}(0.1)^{7-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(5\\le X\\le 6) & =P(X\\in {5,6}) \\\\  & =f(5)+f(6) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{5})(0.9)^{5}(0.1)^{7-5}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{6})(0.9)^{6}(0.1)^{7-6} \\\\  & =21(0.9)^{5}(0.1)^{2}+7(0.9)^{6}(0.1) \\\\  & ≈0.4960\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $4$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362210 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166889 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4844e17-9365-5a6f-a5f4-b9fa83fd81e1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over an Interval Using the Complement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ce615c1-c056-50fa-a609-0af78e4d7278" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9547" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361389} {:answer/id #uuid "90bb771b-acb1-5d7b-82d6-e6cb2a2f76c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9078" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361477} {:answer/id #uuid "ae2222aa-6db4-5123-82d5-43e005e9d05a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9625" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361607} {:answer/id #uuid "eddad30f-5a3e-5f84-ac5e-0779efe69930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9922" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361894} {:answer/id #uuid "f6f90992-a3f4-5ce7-bb9a-05f52ebcdf90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9375" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eddad30f-5a3e-5f84-ac5e-0779efe69930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9922" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361894} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd6feca6-76ee-5295-b8ca-70efe4a6b0da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362154}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cd0fa54f-0d49-5cf3-b205-811b4116ac34" :question/math-academy-id "q-97521" :question/problem "Given that $X∼B(7,0.5),$ compute $P(X\\le 6).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(7,0.5),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{x})(0.5)^{x}(0.5)^{7-x}.\n$$\n\nComputing $P(X\\le 6)$ will require us to compute $f(x)$ at $x=0,1,2,…,6.$ However, if we compute the complement $P(X>6)$ instead, then we will only have to compute $f(x)$ at $x=7,$ which will result in a much faster computation.\n\nComputing $P(X>6),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>6) & =P(X\\in {7}) \\\\  & =f(7) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{7})(0.5)^{7}(0.5)^{7-7} \\\\  & =(0.5)^{7} \\\\  & ≈0.0078\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 6) & =1-P(X>6) \\\\  & ≈1-0.0078 \\\\  & =0.9922\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362243 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083322 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7e9daa2-4aaa-5c33-b611-231f3d0e4782" :knowledge-point/title "Z-Score: Word Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19447168-1474-5be9-aac6-ad97fed12bd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.61" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360937} {:answer/id #uuid "1fe70794-f488-59e6-afbe-8f9bdce41f92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.57" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360972} {:answer/id #uuid "26497c17-1e52-5f13-9672-7d7541e882bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361001} {:answer/id #uuid "2e772d5a-11ce-5758-badf-fef6f326e8c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361044} {:answer/id #uuid "ed84d4c8-52d6-5fed-ade3-9113944fe3ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.57" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fe70794-f488-59e6-afbe-8f9bdce41f92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.57" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360972} :answer-field/id #uuid "a007c32a-4b84-5c4e-9daf-279e1005a6e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362130}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a38c82cd-ff4b-53d8-85bc-f102ff90d451" :question/math-academy-id "q-9785" :question/problem "The mean weight of a group of newborn children is $3.2kg$ with a standard deviation of $350g.$\nWhat is the $z$-score that corresponds to a weight of $3kg?$ Give your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, we need to convert $350g=0.350kg$\n\nThe $z$-score of an observation $x$ is defined by\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma }.\n$$\n\nThe $z$-score represents how many standard deviations an observation is above or below the mean of the data set.\n\nSo, the $z$-score of the observation $x=3$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{x-\\overset{¯}{x}}{\\sigma } \\\\  & =\\frac{3-3.2}{0.350} \\\\  & =-0.57\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nto $2$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395363 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068814 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52e11164-1929-594d-9f9e-460f6469421d" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Stretched Negative Reciprocal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc019953-ecf5-53ce-9d3b-5be9dc928b8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/76/76d779da22082439eb40586ddbeb325368b1f49f15fef3158ebaa0eee90f4d84.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395365} {:answer/id #uuid "d9a9fcde-d13a-59ef-9cbd-e86e49dc3376" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/64/647f80eeaea16048c9ba2677624ff6f3393183f6ea0df1529defa5fd55881f52.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395366} {:answer/id #uuid "384d2a96-99ce-5ac9-943e-ea352fc9b0c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a3/a32495fcca947d01cf8d3218fc5cfc0cce083a34e6fb6b629d92ba9e47039a6e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395367} {:answer/id #uuid "76b6a6b4-55f1-58d8-83a3-1765de08f84e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/66/6681008fc3e783b64e6bce7e7e0e9c72beb8761fc8cebb35926d528aac076bd9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395368} {:answer/id #uuid "5a98192b-2704-5d37-b470-494583da6584" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d5/d5d93e12275c683e0a56f0ec9a0c99540a7774879cd0f8fa69151ddba68a44fa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc019953-ecf5-53ce-9d3b-5be9dc928b8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/76/76d779da22082439eb40586ddbeb325368b1f49f15fef3158ebaa0eee90f4d84.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395365} :answer-field/id #uuid "405bc041-6aa9-5fe5-b160-7fb6f4c24cc5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "37c54602-8f73-5b80-9ca3-ee3a40eda45c" :question/math-academy-id "q-97949" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=-\\frac{3}{4x}?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot $y=-\\frac{3}{4x},$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=-\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/d5/d5d93e12275c683e0a56f0ec9a0c99540a7774879cd0f8fa69151ddba68a44fa.png)\n\n- Stretch the result by a scale factor of $\\frac{3}{4}$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=-\\frac{3}{4x}.$\n\n![](images/76/76d779da22082439eb40586ddbeb325368b1f49f15fef3158ebaa0eee90f4d84.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395370 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068779 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4640f978-9caa-5960-bec4-f153a1b4cc5d" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Transformed Reciprocal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ac99bf6-327a-5511-b7a4-734f9c76a5be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b9/b9683bcc1b559505141d45b9007ef45011adee98b33730ffad1876a462d9abc1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395372} {:answer/id #uuid "d352e845-d658-517b-b4e5-85b07a890741" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/80/804eac3df07358686b859f20630acc5344f82ff0c71dc80e6320ab2f7b2313f2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395373} {:answer/id #uuid "b31d701f-35d2-53fb-9bb7-6e53d677c6e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a2/a2af9de8793d7f2e527721b3874ac6d49d25d0a1e496d3cd62ecf2710b4ab932.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395374} {:answer/id #uuid "91f3d40c-2faf-5526-b31d-531299ba0057" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c5/c52f568868328061237d43e4650180321ec4a40e886a62f4a678f0276afe821b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395375} {:answer/id #uuid "d4ab1f08-3af2-5eaf-b009-717e15cf1eaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/40/40490df9d6ec4cc44759b0265e9355dfe6edba66028e61d6e5c36c0d839388d9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91f3d40c-2faf-5526-b31d-531299ba0057" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c5/c52f568868328061237d43e4650180321ec4a40e886a62f4a678f0276afe821b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395375} :answer-field/id #uuid "7dcbea8f-b000-5d95-8095-fceedf72d8ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b5276417-1d46-5a8a-a5e9-31a83ac43586" :question/math-academy-id "q-98004" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2(x+1)}?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot $y=\\frac{1}{2(x+1)},$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/a3/a32495fcca947d01cf8d3218fc5cfc0cce083a34e6fb6b629d92ba9e47039a6e.png)\n\n- Translate it by $1$ unit to the left to get the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{x+1}.$\n\n![](images/04/049f9c306c007026bc72e5f00d925cf22d80d94049aa82e3c81d7fc7c289249f.png)\n\n- Then, stretch the result by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2(x+1)}.$\n\n![](images/c5/c52f568868328061237d43e4650180321ec4a40e886a62f4a678f0276afe821b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395423 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073814 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4e178c7-bdb4-5139-a20c-3e17de51ee11" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between Two Points With Variable Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81b1c11b-b4b1-536c-8889-3a3eb09882dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}\\,w" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395425} {:answer/id #uuid "c96ad5b8-0210-5052-9081-f410de18a5e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7w" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395426} {:answer/id #uuid "398a8fd3-2eed-529b-9541-d6be85bc82d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6w" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395427} {:answer/id #uuid "04663b1a-f275-5578-853a-d2e34a0425ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}\\,w" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395428} {:answer/id #uuid "16d6cb4d-d2f4-501f-8ae8-ed5949dd56c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}\\,w" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395429}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16d6cb4d-d2f4-501f-8ae8-ed5949dd56c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}\\,w" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395429} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea09eb90-beeb-5d00-94ad-88249fe8aeca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395424}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fb9ffaa1-a202-507f-aa7e-4d95a973e5e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-98142" :question/problem "Calculate the distance between the points $(3w,2w,7w)$ and $(-w,6w,3w),$ given that $w$ is positive." :question/worked-solution "The distance $d$ between two points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1}{)}^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1}{)}^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1}{)}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have ${{(3w,2w,7w)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and ${{(-w,6w,3w)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}}.$ Applying the distance formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(-w-3w{)}^{2}+(6w-2w{)}^{2}+(3w-7w{)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4w{)}^{2}+(4w{)}^{2}+(-4w{)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{w}^{2}+16{w}^{2}+16{w}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{48{w}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{48}\\,\\sqrt{{w}^{2}} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}\\,|w|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $w$ is positive, we have $|w|=w.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nd=4\\sqrt{3}\\,w.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362272 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171913 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7da9cbe0-e57b-5289-be33-c21af3f68ee7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Primary Roots of Unity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a2dcd36-d37e-5f97-a4eb-f4a0e43b3d0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2\\pi \\text{i}/7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361244} {:answer/id #uuid "7a8ef0d3-446a-5d6e-ada9-16f2db45756c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3\\pi \\text{i}/14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361378} {:answer/id #uuid "b8feab6f-aab4-50c9-a460-d7a38f586edc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{7\\pi \\text{i}/5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361661} {:answer/id #uuid "db29ac2c-5b62-5efc-a993-5086c236ea43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi \\text{i}/7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361812} {:answer/id #uuid "fbcd738f-f001-53ce-9a93-cd0317226734" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi \\text{i}/5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a2dcd36-d37e-5f97-a4eb-f4a0e43b3d0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2\\pi \\text{i}/7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361244} :answer-field/id #uuid "96d34327-185f-56e1-8f43-80ae24e1544f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dbbbaf02-7f2f-5863-a78f-3470c810112d" :question/math-academy-id "q-98161" :question/problem "Find the principal $7$th root of unity." :question/worked-solution "Let $z$ be a $7$th root of unity. This means that \n\n$$\n{z}^{7}=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the complex number $1$ in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta }.$ Notice that $|1|=1$ and $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(1)=0.$ Hence, \n\n$$\n1={e}^{\\text{i}(0+2k\\pi )}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is an integer. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{7}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}}\\,⟹\\,z=({e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}})^{1/7}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}/7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe principal root corresponds to $k=1,$ and is therefore equal to ${e}^{2\\pi \\text{i}/7}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362297 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083322 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7e9daa2-4aaa-5c33-b611-231f3d0e4782" :knowledge-point/title "Z-Score: Word Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07de7854-21ae-5eb4-a781-39eaf6920d9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "61.3%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360866} {:answer/id #uuid "11e0c7a1-7e38-5347-b91a-0182668e0eb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65.3%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360905} {:answer/id #uuid "2cca5a8b-b099-5b27-bb51-5a8a76591d49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "63.3%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361037} {:answer/id #uuid "67064f5b-20ba-52d5-8416-dc7de2bf88a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "57.55%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361300} {:answer/id #uuid "6755f482-de75-5ebc-aed7-289483aad1cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "59.05%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361302}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6755f482-de75-5ebc-aed7-289483aad1cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "59.05%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361302} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec9455a6-4ffc-529e-9d61-00633942c052" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362191}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5e2633ed-ce6d-535e-9977-3b543de5d2f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-9846" :question/problem "In a trigonometry test, the class instructor decided that students whose $z$-score is greater than or equal to $-1$ will pass the test. The mean percent score $x$ of the students of the class is $\\overset{¯}{x}=64.3%$ with a standard deviation of $\\sigma =5.25%.$ Find the minimum passing score." :question/worked-solution "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\overset{―}{x}=64.3,$ $z=-1,$ and $\\sigma =5.25$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\  & =64.3+(-1)\\cdot 5.25 \\\\  & =64.3-5.25 \\\\  & =59.05.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the minimum passing score is $59.05%.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362298 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147491 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "66924d52-fd39-5308-b78e-611e3d3129ad" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Line That Corresponds to a Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b5a1453-83df-5991-9fd6-135cf92a1272" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c5/c5e335ffe5f2343671d1f9eca6cedc34ef34a9700837451e963cfcfe1cd153d5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361108} {:answer/id #uuid "3f0f0bcd-a732-5bb7-8af2-fe05f1453089" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/eb/eb621888b04d5bd9b842624c278422995db44097adde61bbc769e8df0a5a47e7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361122} {:answer/id #uuid "5f7dcf80-41db-5be0-ac2e-e22110ddadb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a7/a77fcb19143b7bdee347993a1da4fb6f73c5c1460c55ecab24ffde9afe7f6cf0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361267} {:answer/id #uuid "6fb068f9-4864-5875-ad12-26ac33f5a915" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0b/0b6a1907f045e737c1f136a47d941069efbfd23a389add17d1ae31cf336763b8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361332} {:answer/id #uuid "969fafc8-0546-5321-bf5c-6fff9f7de328" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/39/39ab4bfad5e05067d9e50979deb2a7699eb41aa87037fdf6e3a6d14a98dcfa19.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361508}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "969fafc8-0546-5321-bf5c-6fff9f7de328" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/39/39ab4bfad5e05067d9e50979deb2a7699eb41aa87037fdf6e3a6d14a98dcfa19.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361508} :answer-field/id #uuid "832aff61-5879-5808-944b-77a06173542d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1f99c9bb-9f0b-5721-8c35-4c71f8216676" :question/math-academy-id "q-98525" :question/problem "Which of the following is the phase line of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}+y-2?$" :question/worked-solution "To draw the phase line of the differential equation, we proceed using the following three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation. \n\nIn this case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+y-2 & =0 \\\\ (y-1)(y+2) & =0 \\\\ y & =-2,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-2$ and $y=1.$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the intervals of $y$ for which $f(y)>0$ and $f(y)<0.$ \n\nConstructing a sign table, we get the following:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,1)$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (1,\\infty ),$ and negative for $y\\in (-2,1).$\n\n**Step 3**: Draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y)>0$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y)<0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](images/39/39ab4bfad5e05067d9e50979deb2a7699eb41aa87037fdf6e3a6d14a98dcfa19.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362313 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147494 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "680a42fd-9b70-5fb2-916c-d10b71b11d82" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibrium Points Using Phase Lines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b34748f-857d-5cfa-b351-1a43a20c2692" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361107} {:answer/id #uuid "61cb4512-f982-534e-a91e-870ab396ea10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361275} {:answer/id #uuid "84029e23-38de-5dd2-96bf-7030fa4379e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361425} {:answer/id #uuid "c9562f76-31e9-5fb8-8d05-14359ba8542f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361746} {:answer/id #uuid "e5e5787c-1281-5971-b262-071aa37d5e7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361864}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9562f76-31e9-5fb8-8d05-14359ba8542f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361746} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a69467f-1203-587a-9ced-02e01b856019" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362004}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dd0fe6f6-6555-578d-880a-04a4380b3213" :question/math-academy-id "q-98700" :question/problem "![](images/4c/4c4ef140c85eb91bd01688cc2a447f13a21c2a51964c6d101887d15045cee95d.png)\n\nA particular autonomous differential equation has the phase line shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=-2$ is a sink\n- $y=-2$ is a source\n- $y=2$ is a node" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nLet's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=-2$ points downward, and the arrow just below it points upward. In other words, both arrows point *toward* the point $y=-2.$This means that $y=-2$ is a sink.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=2$ points downward, and the arrow just below it points downward too. In other words, both arrows point in the *same* direction on either side of $y=2.$ This means that $y=2$ is a node.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362319 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147495 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4535b48e-a33f-5f62-95d3-a5c0991c1f6d" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibrium Points Using a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "159d1b6f-bc88-5e81-930b-1feb13f58e60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360922} {:answer/id #uuid "3b043f4e-61a3-5d51-8cc8-0b48259e3f97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361106} {:answer/id #uuid "9cd47953-1f4c-5a18-8e96-037941d6ed94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361535} {:answer/id #uuid "b102e3ce-72af-58f5-a986-ba1591632b13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361624} {:answer/id #uuid "da8cf20a-84a5-5aef-ab67-755e3689bae1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b043f4e-61a3-5d51-8cc8-0b48259e3f97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361106} :answer-field/id #uuid "147b2830-9a9a-5acf-aeaa-a5ee194361a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361997}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d67a28ee-e52e-5e0c-b902-450c792552a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-98715" :question/problem "![](images/4a/4a2c0103fb348883b05692721162ffba17758beb0e6f944deb328a506fed8d21.png)\n\nConsiderer the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=4$ is a source.\n- $y=2$ is a node.\n- $y=-1$ is a sink." :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nThe phase line that corresponds to the given graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/df/df340c40982e6d22ef8a815bf043f84fd78b43b4ddc1763845d03d4b2c793aee.png)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- Statement I is false. From the phase line, we see that $y=4$ is a sink.\n\n- Statement II is false. From the phase line, we see that $y=2$ is a source.\n\n- Statement III is true. From the phase line, we see that $y=-1$ is a sink.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362320 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083334 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41db8918-3463-50ca-af08-d975bde2dbca" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Using the Lowest Common Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "118da805-8d4d-5712-a36c-cd77c1c5bcc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360902} {:answer/id #uuid "221ed9dd-6363-58f5-a13f-28ea35802178" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360987} {:answer/id #uuid "2b8d4542-dea0-5420-b58c-12dbab7429c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361030} {:answer/id #uuid "3432d0b7-a400-5e44-af3e-694272f1758a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361067} {:answer/id #uuid "7608caaa-0a67-5ff3-8a2a-4143e6b8492c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361359}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b8d4542-dea0-5420-b58c-12dbab7429c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361030} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca620ff8-9a8a-5856-bc6f-86f00cb0ed6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362168}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c2197596-2744-5571-ba37-b2f03d9b6d9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-98716" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{1}{x+2}=\\frac{4}{x(x+2)}.$" :question/worked-solution "We make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(x,\\,x+2,\\,x(x+2))=x(x+2).\n$$\n\n- The values $x=0$ and $x=-2$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{1}{x+2} & =\\frac{4}{x(x+2)} \\\\ x(x+2)\\cdot (\\frac{2}{x-1}-\\frac{1}{x+2}) & =x(x+2)\\cdot \\frac{4}{x(x+2)} \\\\ \\frac{2x(x+2)}{x}-\\frac{x(x+2)}{x+2} & =\\frac{4x(x+2)}{x(x+2)} \\\\ \\frac{2\\cancel{x}\\cdot (x+2)}{\\cancel{x}}-\\frac{x\\cdot \\cancel{(x+2)}}{\\cancel{x+2}} & =\\frac{4\\cdot \\cancel{x(x+2)}}{\\cancel{x(x+2)}} \\\\ 2(x+2)-x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $x,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x+2)-x & =4 \\\\ 2x+4-x & =4 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $x=0$ is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362322 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147495 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4535b48e-a33f-5f62-95d3-a5c0991c1f6d" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibrium Points Using a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15c32191-765d-58f7-8e7d-323f392118cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360924} {:answer/id #uuid "46e7e4ee-6254-5172-8b9f-397f96825893" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361145} {:answer/id #uuid "a2bc5784-c1de-50bd-9e9e-2bf05ff09080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361564} {:answer/id #uuid "e0f5b8c2-31d6-5107-96f4-ef046c9625b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361842} {:answer/id #uuid "fa87ecf3-4de2-5513-813c-b3aef6e4035f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361957}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46e7e4ee-6254-5172-8b9f-397f96825893" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361145} :answer-field/id #uuid "43aa3a0a-d08a-5e61-897e-a608c4281c42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362044}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "99cb8df0-a236-544f-967a-a702cdee4d6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-98752" :question/problem "![](images/31/3114d1c94b63a2506a66e701346bc7367b901659b78a2cf7c191648c96a716dc.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=3$ is a node.\n- $y=-1$ is a sink.\n- $y=-1$ is a source." :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nThe phase line that corresponds to the given graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/f7/f72f89ecad74cc708b5950763002757d9d012d3859c7a5bd5eb19ae60fc54e55.png)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- Statement I is false. From the phase line, we see that $y=3$ is a sink.\n\n- Statement II is false while statement III is true. From the phase line, we see that $y=-1$ is a source.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362327 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147496 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b114a373-697f-5005-bb1b-46202547a905" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying the Equilibrium Points of a Given Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11598374-2ed1-553f-b905-50dec6fb60f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=-1$ is a source, and $y=2$ is a sink" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360900} {:answer/id #uuid "25a5ee4f-b94b-5a04-9d46-21eeb82557ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=-1$ and $y=2$ are both sources" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360998} {:answer/id #uuid "34b65b2c-0c3d-5e6b-8d07-095213959dcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=-1$ is a source, and $y=2$ is a node" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361068} {:answer/id #uuid "52c6f174-6f29-5d62-bd5d-5ed9f92e0888" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=-1$ and $y=2$ are both sinks" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361203} {:answer/id #uuid "d40ef267-ad4e-5b80-91e8-57fe6ab69d40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=-1$ is a sink, and $y=2$ is a source" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361787}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11598374-2ed1-553f-b905-50dec6fb60f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=-1$ is a source, and $y=2$ is a sink" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360900} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6616dfe-27ea-567a-9d81-00969792ab51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362138}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e5477d34-1ebb-5af1-ac02-05ea5038d3a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-98762" :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the equilibrium solutions of ${y}^{′}=2+y-{y}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ 2+y-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ -({y}^{2}-y-2) & =0 \\\\ -(y+1)(y-2) & =0 \\\\ y & =-1,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-1$ and $y=2.$ \n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $(-1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-(y+1)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $y-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (2,\\infty ),$ and positive for $y\\in (-1,2).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](images/92/9230d847daa518edfaad43f6552a48c2b3cac9523a1e9ecad8624851080e00ea.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solutions:\n\n- $y=-1$ is a source because the nearby solutions diverge away from this point.\n\n- $y=2$ is a sink because the nearby solutions converge toward this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=-1$ is a source, and $y=2$ is a sink\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395549 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147497 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b58b04cc-da5e-5c51-a583-0663d783c697" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Slope Fields Corresponding to Some Equilibrium Point Classifications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff8dd8d0-7d50-5734-b6ea-496d2a570e8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/57/5737491c0a472a12cb3fd9e0b09cb5ede88b193a3f084ca89671e4669b945d5b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395551} {:answer/id #uuid "d3cc9a9b-5ccf-500e-912e-e1f5b871939d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7d/7df58db225ecb32e6b51b2c16d78a557ca57e63f5d85551dfc1ddc85865e9ea4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395552} {:answer/id #uuid "a7af3a60-0900-581d-aa3e-62e9b0e7848f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4e/4e360e24357a714978fdfb64b239381b3d7c66d13a1307c265f067881aaebc54.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395553} {:answer/id #uuid "26e997a9-c909-5c38-8bf1-5444421cdf7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f1/f1302588da5388ebfa996005675625f98a9edee7c5ec0e99a2c897e70bd7e680.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395554} {:answer/id #uuid "3314cccf-cba5-50b7-a0ea-9fe1a2c4b4ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a7/a7961c00b93091e3bf775f6f61f503d9f3d16ad30174c4f3310654f5aa75721c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395555}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff8dd8d0-7d50-5734-b6ea-496d2a570e8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/57/5737491c0a472a12cb3fd9e0b09cb5ede88b193a3f084ca89671e4669b945d5b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395551} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f774429-ecb7-569b-bb60-6fa8f2847a27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395550}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a90c5eac-a818-5712-a0c3-9443f22bd769" :question/math-academy-id "q-98809" :question/problem "A particular autonomous differential equation has the following properties:\n\n- $y=-2$ is a node\n\n- $y=3$ is a sink\n\nWhich of the following could be the slope field of the differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information. \n\nNote that there are $2$ equilibrium points, $y=-2$ and $y=3.$\n\n- We're given that $y=3$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just above $y=3$ and an upward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=-2$ is a node. This means that the arrows on either side of $y=-2$ point in the same direction. Since the arrow just above $y=-2$ points upward, the arrow just below it points upward too.\n\n![](images/5e/5ea8af3b30df51189c3511dbe093f05b309dc50cbdea1e52d5ba3a42d88101c2.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that correspond with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](images/57/5737491c0a472a12cb3fd9e0b09cb5ede88b193a3f084ca89671e4669b945d5b.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=-2$ and $y=3.$\n\n- The slopes near $y=-2$ appear to diverge away from this line when we're looking above the line and appear to converge toward the line when we're looking below the line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=3$ appear to converge toward this line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404967 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083295 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4bd2a8e-357a-5f22-bd50-608e68432a8d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae8b541b-6c2c-5c34-8f52-8d8bc5c341df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=3$ and $x=5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404969} {:answer/id #uuid "83b7d222-6b21-5f46-baad-0be2919bb086" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404970} {:answer/id #uuid "42221465-d5d3-5322-81e1-4397847a1a65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=-7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404971} {:answer/id #uuid "d6d814eb-c7d7-579f-9607-8d549d7bda4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=-6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404972} {:answer/id #uuid "1b3e5eca-a564-5d66-b064-7f655185e885" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404973}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42221465-d5d3-5322-81e1-4397847a1a65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=-7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404971} :answer-field/id #uuid "20839a78-520c-5f1d-8f7a-a6481c46623d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404968}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8a08e4af-7f63-5439-b490-91f36e22b19e" :question/math-academy-id "q-99032" :question/problem "Find the critical points of the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}+21x+23.$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}+21x+23) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+24x+21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+24x+21 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+8x+7 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x+7) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,\\,-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-1$ and $x=-7.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=-1$ and $x=-7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404974 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083296 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48e49ade-8bfe-56e7-8b52-950fa84e4bb0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points of Exponential Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd281f17-99d6-5be7-a5a7-e5d777ed4a88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404976} {:answer/id #uuid "80c4a5f6-a26f-5884-b87b-97b1c3d00708" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404977} {:answer/id #uuid "1972cef3-5e06-5006-a5e9-98532f424f21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404978} {:answer/id #uuid "82ae3c82-b9bb-5ca5-96e5-fbbe4ef1e350" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404979} {:answer/id #uuid "1df8665d-93b7-5a6b-9a93-0bb764b11fcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-4$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404980}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82ae3c82-b9bb-5ca5-96e5-fbbe4ef1e350" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404979} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a891f36-9832-5e16-a500-7251af8a1737" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404975}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fb13e83e-9cd0-53cb-aaf0-21f542a77f1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-99050" :question/problem "Find the critical points of the function  $f(x)={e}^{12x-{x}^{3}}?$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{12x-{x}^{3}}) \\\\  & ={(12x-{x}^{3})}^{′}{e}^{12x-{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =(12-3{x}^{2}){e}^{12x-{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(12-3{x}^{2}){e}^{12x-{x}^{3}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince ${e}^{12x-{x}^{3}}\\ne 0$ for all $x,$ we can divide this equation by ${e}^{12x-{x}^{3}}$ and solve the resulting equation. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12-3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3(4-{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ (2-x)(2+x) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-2$ and $x=2.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=-2$ and $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404981 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083329 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "772ea6ab-9884-51ef-82df-a9d5cd523719" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points of Rational Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e62997e2-1f0c-5d4b-9eb1-4b41bb6ab374" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404983} {:answer/id #uuid "eaf010c4-f01a-50cf-8d58-06c4c1650590" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404984} {:answer/id #uuid "01c6ef37-e7d0-563b-82d0-0dd75adf8406" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-6$ and $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404985} {:answer/id #uuid "8ca2e0a4-dadf-59ca-94f1-7367e00a0944" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404986} {:answer/id #uuid "ab9a5af9-9a13-510f-84ca-0b15cfce2ca4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-6$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404987}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eaf010c4-f01a-50cf-8d58-06c4c1650590" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404984} :answer-field/id #uuid "6da6d1b2-a92b-5381-b98b-6a6c794e9358" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404982}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "42dd53a1-4ccb-5086-adb5-70b77ded4e6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-99053" :question/problem "What are the critical points of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2x+1}{{x}^{2}+6}?$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2x+1}{{x}^{2}+6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(2x+1)^{′}\\cdot ({x}^{2}+6)-(2x+1)\\cdot ({x}^{2}+6)^{′}}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot ({x}^{2}+6)-(2x+1)\\cdot 2x}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+12-4{x}^{2}-2x}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12-2x-2{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12-2x-2{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+6)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 12-2x-2{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+x-6 & =0 \\\\ (x+3)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-3$ and $x=2.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=-3$ and $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404988 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083329 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "772ea6ab-9884-51ef-82df-a9d5cd523719" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points of Rational Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3099f3c2-846b-5ae3-b13d-62bf2853c8e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404990} {:answer/id #uuid "80bf0b4b-0794-5476-96ae-6da113dacb97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404991} {:answer/id #uuid "1d2185e2-fc3d-5abf-9cc2-33c57fd4ee19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=3$ and $x=\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404992} {:answer/id #uuid "4278a543-dfc0-5d1f-b8c7-13dc3aeb961d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404993} {:answer/id #uuid "7ea262a0-90f2-5ce8-828c-598aa9e08bf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ and $x=5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404994}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ea262a0-90f2-5ce8-828c-598aa9e08bf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ and $x=5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404994} :answer-field/id #uuid "25281d74-3b09-5c83-b4d4-d24247da42a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404989}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "63c773de-f51b-54ba-8cf5-768e0048eb88" :question/math-academy-id "q-99055" :question/problem "What are the critical points of the function $f(x)=\\frac{x-3}{{x}^{2}-5}?$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{x-3}{{x}^{2}-5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-3)^{′}\\cdot ({x}^{2}-5)-(x-3)\\cdot ({x}^{2}-5)^{′}}{({x}^{2}-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}-5)-(x-3)\\cdot 2x}{({x}^{2}-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5+6x-{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}-6x+5}{({x}^{2}-5)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x$ by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{{x}^{2}-6x+5}{({x}^{2}-5)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+5 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}-{3}^{2}+5 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-9+5 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =1,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=1$ and $x=5.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist at $x=\\pm \\sqrt{5}.$ But these points do not lie in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=1$ and $x=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404995 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083329 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "772ea6ab-9884-51ef-82df-a9d5cd523719" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points of Rational Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "065b8702-9c1f-5408-a110-0b6b1f931f94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404997} {:answer/id #uuid "7e04b74f-d9c6-59e7-a8ee-004d2123563f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404998} {:answer/id #uuid "625ac297-0116-50dc-b32e-593a5c25fd5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404999} {:answer/id #uuid "2f10324a-c1cb-5f86-99bd-f736b0ed8050" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-\\sqrt{2}$ and $x=\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405000} {:answer/id #uuid "acea3269-c2cc-5d39-87be-8ac3b32540bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405001}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e04b74f-d9c6-59e7-a8ee-004d2123563f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404998} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcf1d3ea-9362-58c4-b795-b8796b5019b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404996}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "70a98919-dd4e-535e-b918-4ce91d1560b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-99056" :question/problem "What are the critical points of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2x-3}{{x}^{2}-2}?$" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2x-3}{{x}^{2}-2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(2x-3)^{′}\\cdot ({x}^{2}-2)-(2x-3)\\cdot ({x}^{2}-2)^{′}}{({x}^{2}-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot ({x}^{2}-2)-(2x-3)\\cdot 2x}{({x}^{2}-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4+6x-2{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2{x}^{2}-6x+4}{({x}^{2}-2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2{x}^{2}-6x+4}{({x}^{2}-2)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-6x+4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x+2 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =1,\\,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=1$ and $x=2.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist at $x=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.$ But these points do not lie in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=1$ and $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069542 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068813 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea2f1348-55bf-5313-8d5e-a5a097231e97" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Stretched Reciprocal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a4dc8da-0af6-521a-94e2-29efe502a4e